lves would be the first to  say that  um i'm pleased that the prime minister  is taking a lead on this  i'm pleased that the emphasis now and  that commitment is  to put children and put in schools at  the heart of the  opening up of the economy and opening up  of the country  and i think that's where it needs to be  because it's much more complex than  just what goes on in schools a whole  range of factors need to be in place  to ensure that schools open and that now  must be the priority  and just finally research by the joseph  rowntree foundation this week has found  that over four million children  living in poverty one million of them  under the age of given this would you  like to see the 20 pound a week  uplift to universal credit extended  yes i think it does need to be extended  it's had an important impact and that  needs to continue  this isn't the time for families to have  that uncertainty  or that drop in income and what we do  know with very young children that  clearly  are bearing some of the brunt of that  increase in poverty  if you fall behind before you get to  school you have very few chances  to catch up during your school day so  the impact of  on a child's life throughout so it is so  important i think it's  clear now we have a problem with poverty  in this country  we really need a long-term plan as part  of that buildback  to ensure that all children can flourish  and all cam families can give their  children  the support they want to okay anne  longfield thank you so much for  coming on the program this morning thank  you
 

